<h2>A marriage, a panchayat, and two criminal cases</h2>
<p>The complainant and the first petitioner are husband and wife whose marriage had collapsed. On the husband&rsquo;s account, a panchayat was held, he paid Rs 10,51,000 to the wife, and the panchayat advised divorce; he then filed a divorce case before the Family Court, Darbhanga. The wife lodged an FIR under Section 498A IPC and allied provisions against him and his family. The husband answered with a criminal complaint of his own against the wife and eight of her relatives, alleging assault, wrongful restraint, theft and extortion-related offences. The petitioners denied that the panchayat payment was ever received. It is this counter-complaint in which cognizance was taken, summons issued and — when the petitioners say the summons never reached them — a bailable warrant followed.</p>

<h2>Why the court declined to decide the merits</h2>
<p>Counsel for the petitioners pressed two points: that the process had escalated to a warrant without service of summons, and that magistrates sometimes remand accused persons to custody mechanically on appearance, notwithstanding Supreme Court directions governing summons-stage matters. The Additional Public Prosecutor&rsquo;s answer was structural: cognizance has been taken, only a bailable warrant exists, and if the petitioners appear before the trial court they will not be arrested — a bailable warrant by itself furnishes no reasonable apprehension of arrest. The State placed reliance on the Supreme Court&rsquo;s order dated 23 April 2026 in <em>Om Prakash Chhawnika v. State of Jharkhand</em>.</p>
<p>The court recorded that it was inclined to grant anticipatory bail, but on the petitioners&rsquo; own stand that no non-bailable warrant had been issued till date, it disposed of the application: the petitioners are to appear before the trial court on or before 3 December 2026, carrying a copy of the Supreme Court&rsquo;s order, and the trial court is to proceed strictly in accordance with it.</p>

<h2>The ladder of coercive process</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Summons</strong> — the ordinary first step in a complaint case; appearance answers it completely.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Bailable warrant</strong> — issued when summons fail; the person appears and is released on furnishing bail endorsed on the warrant. Custody is not its object.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Non-bailable warrant</strong> — the stage at which apprehension of arrest becomes real, and pre-arrest bail jurisdiction is meaningfully engaged.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Proclamation</strong> — continued absence invites proceedings that can disentitle the person from discretionary relief altogether.</div>
</div>
<p>Anticipatory bail — Section 438 CrPC, now Section 482 BNSS — sits alongside this ladder, not on top of it. Filing a pre-arrest bail application at the bailable-warrant stage asks the High Court to protect against an arrest the process does not yet threaten. The practical cost is real: an application held premature consumes months, and the accused must still appear below.</p>

<h2>Lessons for both sides of a matrimonial cross-case</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Read the order sheet before choosing the remedy.</strong> Whether the process stands at summons, bailable warrant or non-bailable warrant decides whether the correct step is appearance with a bail application, a recall application, or a pre-arrest bail plea.</li>
  <li><strong>Appear with the governing directions in hand.</strong> The court here expressly armed the petitioners with the Supreme Court&rsquo;s order and bound the trial court to it — a template worth following wherever mechanical remand on appearance is the fear.</li>
  <li><strong>Disclose antecedents fully.</strong> The petitioners&rsquo; mixed records were put on the table; candour costs less than discovery.</li>
  <li><strong>Treat cross-complaints as independent cases.</strong> A counter-complaint does not dissolve because it follows a 498A FIR, and vice versa; each must be defended on its own record.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> In complaint cases, the window between a bailable warrant and a non-bailable warrant is the cheapest point of repair. An appearance with counsel, bail furnished on the spot, and a short application explaining non-service of summons will ordinarily close the matter — preserving the anticipatory bail remedy, unspent, for the day coercive process genuinely looms.</p>
</div>
